winemac: Keep the outgoing hosted layer until its replacement takes over.

A client that resizes does not resize its swapchain in place: it destroys the
whole client surface and builds a new one.  Measured on steam's client with
WINEDEBUG=trace+macdrv, a single resize step produces

  2  macdrv_client_surface_destroy
  4  remote layer messages:  RELEASE(old) ... CREATE(new) ... RELEASE(old)

so the sequence on the hosting side is release-then-create, and the new
CALayerHost hosts nothing until the client presents its first frame at the new
size.  removeCALayerHostView: dropped the outgoing host out of the layer tree
immediately, which left the window with no layer at all across that gap: one
black frame per resize step, and an interactive drag turns that into visible
strobing.

Retain the outgoing host in the layer tree instead of dropping it.  A new host
is added as a sublayer above it, so the stale frame is only ever visible
through the replacement's content-less period and is covered as soon as the
first new frame lands.  It is dropped on the next release, on setRetinaMode
(where a stale frame at the wrong scale would be worse than none) and on
dealloc, so at most one is ever retained.

This supersedes an earlier attempt that parked a copy of the last drawable in
the container layer on the sending side: that treated a resize as a geometry
change on a persistent layer, which is not what happens, and left a ghost
frame behind wherever the live layer did not cover the container.

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
index 1de9f0a..949c175 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
@@ -394,6 +394,7 @@ @interface WineContentView : WineBaseView <NSTextInputClient, NSViewLayerContent
 
     WineMetalView *_metalView;
     NSMutableDictionary<NSNumber*, CALayerHost*>* _caLayerHosts;
+    CALayerHost* _retiredCALayerHost;
 }
 
 @property (readonly, nonatomic) BOOL everHadGLContext;
@@ -518,6 +519,8 @@ - (void) dealloc
         [markedText release];
         [glContexts release];
         [pendingGlContexts release];
+        [_retiredCALayerHost removeFromSuperlayer];
+        [_retiredCALayerHost release];
         [_caLayerHosts release];
         CGImageRelease(colorImage);
         CGImageRelease(shapeImage);
@@ -742,11 +745,34 @@ - (void) addCALayerHostViewWithContextId:(CAContextID)contextId
         [(WineWindow*)self.window windowDidDrawContent];
     }
 
+    /* A client that resizes releases its old context BEFORE publishing the
+       replacement, and the replacement hosts nothing until it presents its
+       first frame.  Dropping the outgoing host here therefore leaves the
+       window with no layer at all for the width of that gap -- one black
+       frame per resize step, which an interactive drag turns into strobing.
+       Keep the outgoing host in the tree instead.  A newly added host is
+       stacked above it, so the stale frame is only ever visible through the
+       replacement's content-less period, and it is dropped on the next
+       release, on setRetinaMode, and on dealloc.  At most one is retained. */
     - (void) removeCALayerHostView:(CAContextID)contextId
     {
         NSNumber* key = @(contextId);
-        [[_caLayerHosts objectForKey:key] removeFromSuperlayer];
+        CALayerHost* host = [[_caLayerHosts objectForKey:key] retain];
+
         [_caLayerHosts removeObjectForKey:key];
+        if (!host) return;
+
+        [_retiredCALayerHost removeFromSuperlayer];
+        [_retiredCALayerHost release];
+        _retiredCALayerHost = host;
+    }
+
+    /* for the paths that must not leave a stale frame behind */
+    - (void) discardRetiredCALayerHost
+    {
+        [_retiredCALayerHost removeFromSuperlayer];
+        [_retiredCALayerHost release];
+        _retiredCALayerHost = nil;
     }
 
     - (void) setLayerRetinaProperties:(BOOL)mode
@@ -790,6 +816,9 @@ - (void) setRetinaMode:(BOOL)mode
 
         for (CALayerHost* host in [_caLayerHosts allValues])
             host.contentsScale = mode ? 2.0 : 1.0;
+        /* a retired host cannot be rescaled meaningfully, and a stale frame at
+           the wrong scale is worse than none */
+        [self discardRetiredCALayerHost];
 
         [super setRetinaMode:mode];
     }
